Contraceptive Counselling and Prescription
General Practice Other
Request for pregnancy prevention, Screening for hormonal contraindications, Measurement of BP/BMI, Discussion of options/side effects

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-009
Reason for Visit: Acute severe migraine with neurological aura in a patient actively taking the combined oral contraceptive pill, requiring immediate medical intervention and cessation counselling.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young female experiencing a sudden, severe headache accompanied by visual field loss and unilateral numbness, while currently prescribed an oestrogen-containing contraceptive. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out an acute cerebrovascular event, manage the acute neurological symptoms, and mandate the immediate cessation of the combined pill due to absolute contraindication. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a severe, unacceptable risk of ischemic stroke overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "My vision suddenly went jagged and blind on the right side, my hand went numb, and now I have a pounding headache. I take the pill every day."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an acute neurological episode while utilizing reversible prevention of pregnancy. One hour ago, the patient developed a classic visual aura (scintillating scotoma) followed by transient right upper limb paresthesia, which has now evolved into a severe, unilateral, throbbing headache with photophobia. The patient specifically requested continuation of pregnancy prevention. A rapid screening for absolute contraindications to combined hormonal contraceptives confirmed a new-onset migraine with aura, representing a hard stop for oestrogen therapy. A detailed assessment of menstrual history was obtained. The patient expresses severe anxiety regarding both the neurological symptoms and the immediate loss of reliable contraception.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently lying in a dark room due to severe photophobia and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is highly anxious regarding the abrupt cessation of contraception and the fear of an unplanned pregnancy.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Distressed, wearing sunglasses indoors, clutching the right side of the head.
Physical Exam: Neurological: GCS 15. Cranial nerves grossly intact. Visual fields are now full to confrontation (aura has resolved). Power and sensation are 5/5 and symmetrical in all limbs. No cerebellar signs. Cardiovascular: Normal heart sounds, no carotid bruits. The clinical encounter mandated a request for pregnancy prevention alternatives, screening for hormonal contraindications, measurement of BP/BMI, and a discussion of options and side effects. Expected investigation results include completely normal baseline blood pressure and BMI documented prior to prescribing alternative non-oestrogen contraceptives.
Environmental Observations: Darkened room, low noise environment optimized for migraine recovery.
Medication Reconciliation: Ethinylestradiol/Levonorgestrel 30mcg/150mcg daily accessed and verified.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Contraceptive Counselling and Prescription (Immediate Cessation due to Migraine with Aura) - Status: Stable (Neurologically resolving)
Differentials:

Transient Ischemic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The gradual, spreading nature of the sensory symptoms (march) followed by a classic throbbing headache and photophobia, which is characteristic of migraine rather than the sudden vascular deficit of a TIA.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, supple neck with no nuchal rigidity, and absence of a non-blanching petechial rash.

Tension Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct presence of a preceding visual and sensory aura, and the unilateral, throbbing nature of the pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with acute, transient focal neurological deficits followed by a severe headache, diagnostic of a migraine with aura. Because the patient is actively taking a combined oral contraceptive pill, this presentation represents a sudden, absolute medical contraindication due to a significantly elevated risk of ischemic stroke. Urgent face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a detailed neurological examination to rule out an active stroke, administer acute migraine abortive therapy, and enforce the immediate cessation of the oestrogen-containing medication. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have allowed the patient to take their next scheduled pill, exposing them to an unacceptable and immediate cerebrovascular risk.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg and Ondansetron 4mg. Confiscated the remaining combined oral contraceptive pills. Conducted a discussion of patient preferences regarding daily adherence versus long-acting reversible options. Prescribed a Progesterone-Only Pill (Norethisterone) as a safe immediate bridge, with counselling regarding potential side effects such as breakthrough bleeding.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure contraceptive safety moving forward, immediately discard all remaining packets of the combined pill into the household trash tonight. By physically throwing the dangerous medication away, you completely eliminate the risk of accidental ingestion and reinforce the absolute necessity of transitioning to the newly prescribed, oestrogen-free alternative.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to discuss Long-Acting Reversible Contraception (LARC) options and monitor migraine frequency.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you develop sudden facial drooping, an inability to raise both arms, slurred speech, or if the numbness returns and does not resolve within 60 minutes.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The headache is expected to resolve overnight with sleep and simple analgesia. You must use barrier methods (condoms) for the next 7 days while the new progesterone-only pill takes effect.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate emergency medical care if the headache becomes the worst you have ever experienced in your life, or if you begin vomiting uncontrollably.
